{
  "description": "issue #847 regression: `clear_screen`'s homing MUST be observable in the trace stream, so a consumer that replays events agrees with the runtime instead of silently disagreeing with it. This is the exact reproduction from the issue: with a single turtle and no optional profiles, `forward 10` / `right 45` / `clear_screen` used to emit only `clear{mode:\"clear_screen\"}`, leaving a replaying consumer at [0 10] heading 45 while the `print pos` / `print heading` that follow report [0 0] and 0. Now the homing emits `move` ([0 10] -> [0 0], reporting the pre-reset heading 45 exactly as `home` does) then `turn` (45 -> 0) before the `clear`, so a fold of the events and the runtime's own state agree on both position and heading — the requirement in spec/turtles-and-sprites.md:113 that implementations produce trace events with the appropriate turtle identity so animation, stepping, `why`, and `debug` can explain which turtle moved or changed. The pen is DOWN throughout (the program never lifts it) and yet NO `draw-segment` accompanies the homing `move`: `clear_screen` has just wiped the drawing, so a line back to the origin would be exactly the artifact the clear removes (spec/rendering.md's \"Clear operations\" table leaves `clear_screen` with no drawing segments). `move` and `draw-segment` are independent event kinds, so \"moved but drew nothing\" is expressible with no renderer-side special case — contrast the `forward 10` at seq 1/2, where the same pen state DOES produce a `draw-segment`.",
